{"beat": 33, "name": "WHY A RAISE THAT LOSES UNITS CAN PAY", "text": "Here's why it matters more than it looks. When you raise a price, the extra lands in your margin almost whole. Your landed cost doesn't change. Most of your fees don't change. Only the ones charged as a share of the price take a cut of it. So a raise that loses units can still make more money, as long as it doesn't lose too many. How many you can afford to lose depends only on how much of each sale you keep after landed cost and fees. How many you will lose is the slope. On this demo catalogue, that's 38.5%. The gross margin, before fees, is 70.7%. Price off the wrong one and you'll aim at the wrong target. And the slope decides the shape of what comes next. Steeper than minus one, a raise loses a bigger share of units than it adds to the price, so profit rises, peaks and falls: there's a top. Shallower than minus one, a raise loses a smaller share of units than it adds, so profit keeps climbing: no top at all. Remember Woolworth's soap and his skimmers: same price, very different margins, very different answers.", "provider": "placeholder", "words": [{"word": "Here's", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.3}, {"word": "why", "start": 0.3, "end": 0.5}, {"word": "it", "start": 0.5, "end": 0.64}, {"word": "matters", "start": 0.64, "end": 1.06}, {"word": "more", "start": 1.06, "end": 1.4}, {"word": "than", "start": 1.4, "end": 1.62}, {"word": "it", "start": 1.62, "end": 1.74}, {"word": "looks.", "start": 1.74, "end": 2.0}, {"word": "When", "start": 2.62, "end": 2.72}, {"word": "you", "start": 2.72, "end": 2.86}, {"word": "raise", "start": 2.86, "end": 3.12}, {"word": "a", "start": 3.12, "end": 3.3}, {"word": "price,", "start": 3.3, "end": 3.66}, {"word": "the", "start": 4.06, "end": 4.16}, {"word": "extra", "start": 4.16, "end": 4.54}, {"word": "lands", "start": 4.54, "end": 4.94}, {"word": "in", "start": 4.94, "end": 5.1}, {"word": "your", "start": 5.1, "end": 5.2}, {"word": "margin", "start": 5.2, "end": 5.64}, 